window.MOB3_SEARCH = [{"path":"index.html","title":"MOB³ is a small indie web browser for the terminal","text":"MOB³ is a small indie web browser for the terminal MOB³ is a lightweight terminal web browser with a built-in HTML rendering engine, JavaScript runtime, ANSI renderer and inline image support. It brings Vim-style interaction, mouse controls and browser-like navigation together with headless MCP support for scripts and AI agents. The design goal is most of the features people actually use, with a fraction of the CPU and memory a mainstream browser needs: a small static binary, fast startup and pages that stay lightweight. Project page: mob3.net GitHub: github.com/mob3net/mob3 Documentation Documentation index Use cases — developers, AI agents, terminal users and automation Install — releases, Docker and source builds Command line — every flag, default and limit Keyboard and mouse Automation with MCP — headless control for scripts and AI tools Security and privacy , site support and development Try it with Docker: docker run --rm -it ghcr.io/mob3net/mob3 -graphics blocks Download and install Download the latest MOB³ release from GitHub Releases. Release archives cover Linux ( 386 , amd64 , armv6 , armv7 , arm64 ) and macOS ( amd64 , arm64 ). Choose the archive for your OS and architecture, for example mob3_vX.Y.Z_linux_amd64.tar.gz . Each archive contains the static mob3 binary. Example: tar -xzf mob3_vX.Y.Z_linux_amd64.tar.gz sudo install -m 0755 mob3 /usr/local/bin/mob3 mob3 -version Screenshots Show your support If MOB³ has been useful to you, please consider giving the project a star on GitHub . It helps others discover it and supports the continued development of the project. What to expect MOB³ is a content browser, not a full browser engine. It is happiest with text-focused pages and gets rough on modern JavaScript-heavy apps. Works well Documentation, blogs, news articles, wikis, forums, changelogs and old or server-rendered HTML. Headings, paragraphs, lists, links, tables, <pre> blocks, images and common presentational markup. Text/search inputs, textareas, single-select option popups and multi-select list boxes, with a visible caret, arrow-key editing, date/time/month/week, range sliders, color swatches, HTML constraint checks, GET and urlencoded POST form submission. The start page has justified text and a full-width search/URL box that resolves input the same way as the o prompt, including the -search fallback. Common CSS: selectors, cascade and inheritance, box model (including box-sizing and min/max sizes), margins, borders, background colors and background-image photos on block containers, text styling, lists, tables, CSS custom properties, viewport-width @media queries, viewport units, simple calc() , basic flex rows with alignment and ordering, left/right floats with line wrapping and clear , and overflow clipping for bounded panels. PNG/JPEG/GIF/WebP/BMP/SVG/data-URI images when enabled with -graphics ( auto , kitty , iterm , or blocks for half-block art); press i to cycle full graphics, blocks and none. Images are off by default for speed. Session-only memory cache for CSS, scripts and decoded images, with a 32 MiB default budget and -cache to change or disable it. In-memory back/forward cache: Back and Forward restore the page, its DOM and its JavaScript state without a network reload and pause its timers while cached. The cache is memory-aware — a -history-cache budget (128 MB by default) evicts the least recently used pages by estimated size, and pages heavier than half the budget are not cached at all — so heavy sites cannot pin memory. Nothing is written to disk. Light JavaScript: DOM queries and mutation, classList / style , click handlers, timers, alert/confirm/prompt and native <dialog> open/close, same-origin fetch / XMLHttpRequest plus simple CORS cross-origin requests, and in-memory storage/cookies. Script errors turn the footer JavaScript label into a red JavaScript:Error ; J or clicking that label toggles scripting for the session and reloads the page, while ! opens the console. JavaScript console ( ! ): tabbed view of logs, requests, cookies and in-memory storage, the raw page HTML, and a Tools tab for read-only page inspection ( help , form , links , requests , logs , source , snapshot , history , clear ); evaluate one-line expressions in the page's runtime, with Up/Down or Ctrl-P/Ctrl-N history and results appended to the log. clear / cls empties either the JS log or the Tools scrollback. Reader mode ( R , the footer Reader:on / Reader:off label, or -reader at launch): extracts the main article into a narrow, chrome-free text column, stays enabled across navigation, and restores the normal page and scroll position when toggled off. Built-in text-only PDF viewer: a PDF response is extracted page by page into one continuous document with column alignment, a centered reading column and a Page n caption above an underscore rule after each page; every page is padded to the same height so it scrolls like a book, and two-column pages are read column by column. PDF headings keep their spacing, and runs clearly larger than the body text use the same 32px headline path (two rows, scaled glyphs in kitty) as HTML headings. Find, selection, -dump and -png work as on any page. The footer shows Page n/N with a clickable (d -> Download name) hint, and d opens the same curl popup. -png exports one page ( -pdf-page=N or all for the whole document). Figures, images and annotations are not rendered; -no-pdf restores the download prompt. Dark mode ( D , the footer Dark:on / Dark:off label, or -dark at launch): asks sites for their dark styles with prefers-color-scheme: dark . Pages that already render a dark background keep their own palette; otherwise backgrounds, text, links and borders are remapped to a dark palette. Images keep their original colors. Page zoom ( + / - , 0 resets, or -zoom N at launch): changes how many CSS pixels a cell represents, so fixed-pixel layout, spacing, images and kitty scaled large headings grow or shrink while body text keeps its size and line height. The CSS viewport follows the ratio, so responsive pages reflow. Zoom is session-only; the URL row shows the current level, and clicking that badge opens a window for choosing a preset with the mouse. Vimium-style link hints ( f ): highlights letters from each visible link or form control, then follows the link or activates the control when you type them; F shows the destination highlighted in yellow first and waits for confirmation; Esc or scrolling dismisses the hints. Headless MCP automation ( -mcp ): navigate, snapshot page text and links, click links or elements by text, list and fill forms, select options, submit, wait for async content, type, press keys, scroll, evaluate JavaScript, inspect history and read request logs, JS errors or page source. Screen captures are terminal-cell rasters; sessions are memory-only. Browser-style toolbar with back/forward/reload/home, history/help buttons, a bookmarks button when -bookmarks is supplied, a config gear and a centered page title. Keyboard and mouse navigation, including the kitty keyboard protocol in kitty, WezTerm, Ghostty and foot, a standard Tab order through links and form controls (Space/Enter activate, arrows or h/j/k/l change selects, radios and ranges), / incremental find (including multi-word phrases) with highlighted matches (also in the console HTML tab), a draggable page scrollbar, history (including a top-right History page listing the session's links, with # anchors as entries and back/forward restoring each page's last scroll position), bookmarks imported with -bookmarks from a Netscape HTML export, scrolling, focus, link hover targets and CSS/JS hover menus, text selection ( y copies selected text, or the current URL when nothing is selected), fixed-overlay hiding ( X ), and a ! JavaScript console. Rough edges Modern JavaScript apps (SPAs, dashboards, Google search and similar) may show a partial page, a blank page or their no-JavaScript fallback. ES module scripts run, including relative imports, dynamic import() , import.meta.url and bare specifiers resolved by the page's import map, and custom elements are constructed, but the rest of the JavaScript API surface is still a small subset. Reader mode is heuristic: it can pick too little or too much content on unusual layouts, shows a snapshot rather than live updates, and removes images, forms and other interactive controls. Dark mode keeps images in their original colors and approximates some author color schemes. Anti-bot and firewall checks (Cloudflare, CAPTCHA, DataDome/PerimeterX, Imperva/Sucuri, access-denied pages) are generally not usable. A “Site blocked” page is shown instead. No video/audio playback, WebGL, WebAssembly, workers, WebSockets, iframes, Shadow DOM, custom elements, real observers or full History API. Basic CSS grid rows are supported, along with position:relative , position:absolute and position:fixed , left/right floats with line wrapping and clear , and overflow clipping for bounded boxes. position:sticky , z-index , CSS variables, animations, transitions, generated content, background gradients and repeating background tiles are unsupported or approximated as block flow. SVG support is partial: paths, shapes, gradients, transforms, clipPath , fill-rule and <use> of paths/groups work, but text inside SVG, <use> resolving to <symbol> sprites, filters, masks, CSS-in-SVG and animation are not rendered. Animated WebP is not supported. Oversized images are capped at 40% of the viewport width and 16 rows, preserving their aspect ratio. Images with a definite CSS box (both width and height) use object-fit / object-position and fill or crop into that box instead of hitting those caps. No tabs or automatic downloads; a download link opens a popup with a copyable curl command without fetching it, except a .pdf download, which opens in the text viewer ( -no-pdf restores the popup for PDFs), and multipart/file uploads are not implemented (clicking a file input shows an unsupported notice). Selected page text copies with y through OSC 52 (terminal support required), and the URL can be selected with the terminal. Very large pages, stylesheets and images are capped to keep memory and CPU bounded. Style and layout also run under a time budget, so a pathological page is shown partially with a truncation notice instead of freezing the browser. Pages whose timers update the DOM are redrawn a few times per second rather than continuously, and an idle page with no pending timers does not repaint at all. Using command line options Start reading Wikipedia in dark mode: mob3 -dark -reader 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vim_(text_editor)' See all available options with -h flag. Every flag and default is listed in the command-line reference . Capture a PNG screenshot: mob3 -png -width 120 bbc.com/news > bbc.png Automation (MCP) mob3 -mcp runs a headless browser as a Model Context Protocol server over stdin/stdout. It accepts an optional starting URL and fixed -width : { \"mcpServers\": { \"mob3\": { \"command\": \"/path/to/mob3\", \"args\": [\"-mcp\", \"-width\", \"100\", \"about:home\"] } } } Tools: navigate , snapshot , screenshot , click (link number, CSS selector or x / y ), type , press , scroll , back , forward , reload , evaluate and history . snapshot returns the current page viewport as text, visible links as [n] and form controls with coordinates. Results are text only: there is no pixel screenshot because MOB3 renders to terminal cells. The server is local and unauthenticated, sessions and storage are memory-only, and nothing is written to disk. Do not expose it as a network service. See the full MCP reference for the protocol, snapshot format, tool details, and setup instructions for OpenCode, Claude Code, Cursor and Claude Desktop. Development Build from source and start browsing: make ./bin/mob3 https://example.com Requires Go 1.27 or newer. Interactive mode runs on Linux and macOS terminals; Windows users can run it under WSL. When stdin/stdout are not a terminal, MOB³ automatically falls back to dump mode. Press ? inside the app for keys, mouse and forms help. Run mob3 -h for the full list of command-line flags. mob3 -version prints the embedded version. Contributing Contributions are welcome. Every pull request must include the broad license-grant CLA statement described in CONTRIBUTING.md ; it keeps MOB³ available under both AGPL-3.0-only and a commercial license. The PR workflow checks the statement. The CI workflow runs formatting, vet and tests. License MOB³ is licensed under the GNU Affero General Public License, version 3 only (AGPL-3.0-only). Copyright (c) 2026 Milos Stojanovic. See LICENSE . A separate commercial license is available on request for use without the AGPL's source-disclosure requirements. MOB³ is provided “as is”, without warranty of any kind. See the warning at the top: this is not a security boundary. Third-party module licenses are listed in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES .","headings":[{"id":"documentation","text":"Documentation"},{"id":"try-it-with-docker","text":"Try it with Docker:"},{"id":"download-and-install","text":"Download and install"},{"id":"screenshots","text":"Screenshots"},{"id":"show-your-support","text":"Show your support"},{"id":"what-to-expect","text":"What to expect"},{"id":"using-command-line-options","text":"Using command line options"},{"id":"automation-mcp","text":"Automation (MCP)"},{"id":"development","text":"Development"},{"id":"contributing","text":"Contributing"},{"id":"license","text":"License"}]},{"path":"docs/automation.html","title":"Automation with MCP — MOB³ documentation","text":"Automation with MCP mob3 -mcp runs MOB³ headlessly as a Model Context Protocol server over stdin/stdout. It exposes the browser to scripts and AI agents through a small set of text-oriented tools. mob3 -mcp mob3 -mcp -width 100 about:home mob3 -mcp -width 120 https://en.wikipedia.org/ Optional starting URL; otherwise about:home . -width controls wrapping and coordinates. The virtual viewport is 30 rows by default; -height N sets it explicitly (about N-3 page rows after chrome). Images are disabled. -no-js , -layout-budget , -reader , -dark , bookmarks and search settings are honored. History, cookies and storage are in memory only. Client configuration MOB³ speaks MCP over stdio, so any MCP client that can start a local command can use it. The examples below assume mob3 is installed at /usr/local/bin/mob3 ; replace that path with the output of command -v mob3 on your machine (for example /opt/homebrew/bin/mob3 on Apple Silicon). All clients accept the same server arguments. -width controls wrapping and coordinates, and the optional starting URL defaults to about:home : /usr/local/bin/mob3 -mcp -width 100 about:home OpenCode Add MOB³ as a global local server: opencode mcp add mob3 --global -- /usr/local/bin/mob3 -mcp -width 100 about:home opencode mcp list Or add it by hand to ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json (use opencode.json in a project for project-local access): { \"$schema\": \"https://opencode.ai/config.json\", \"mcp\": { \"servers\": { \"mob3\": { \"type\": \"local\", \"command\": [\"/usr/local/bin/mob3\", \"-mcp\", \"-width\", \"100\", \"about:home\"] } } } } OpenCode reloads configuration automatically; opencode mcp list shows the connection state. Claude Code Add MOB³ at user scope so it is available in every project: claude mcp add --transport stdio --scope user mob3 -- \\ /usr/local/bin/mob3 -mcp -width 100 about:home claude mcp list For a project-scoped server, put the equivalent entry in .mcp.json at the project root: { \"mcpServers\": { \"mob3\": { \"command\": \"/usr/local/bin/mob3\", \"args\": [\"-mcp\", \"-width\", \"100\", \"about:home\"] } } } Cursor Create .cursor/mcp.json in the project, or ~/.cursor/mcp.json for a global server: { \"mcpServers\": { \"mob3\": { \"command\": \"/usr/local/bin/mob3\", \"args\": [\"-mcp\", \"-width\", \"100\", \"about:home\"] } } } Reload the MCP servers from Cursor's settings after editing the file. Claude Desktop Add the server to Claude Desktop's configuration file ( claude_desktop_config.json ): { \"mcpServers\": { \"mob3\": { \"command\": \"/usr/local/bin/mob3\", \"args\": [\"-mcp\", \"-width\", \"100\", \"about:home\"] } } } Restart Claude Desktop after changing the file. Other clients Clients that use the common mcpServers shape can use the same entry shown for Cursor. The only required fields are command and args ; the server needs no environment variables, URL or API key. MCP communication is stdio-only. Stdout carries protocol messages only; diagnostics go to stderr. Do not expose the process as a network service; there is no authentication. Protocol Messages are newline-delimited JSON-RPC 2.0. {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\",\"params\":{\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-06-18\"}} {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"method\":\"notifications/initialized\",\"params\":{}} {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":2,\"method\":\"tools/list\",\"params\":{}} {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":3,\"method\":\"tools/call\",\"params\":{\"name\":\"navigate\",\"arguments\":{\"url\":\"https://example.com/\"}}} Supported methods: initialize — returns capabilities.tools , serverInfo and short usage instructions. ping — empty result. tools/list — tool names, descriptions and JSON schemas. tools/call — runs one tool. notifications/initialized and other notifications — acknowledged by silence. Tool failures are returned as successful JSON-RPC responses whose content explains the error and whose isError field is true . Protocol errors use standard JSON-RPC error codes. You can test the server without an MCP client: printf '%s\\n' \\ '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\",\"params\":{\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-06-18\"}}' \\ '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":2,\"method\":\"tools/call\",\"params\":{\"name\":\"navigate\",\"arguments\":{\"url\":\"https://example.com/\"}}}' \\ | ./bin/mob3 -mcp Debugging in the running browser The interactive browser's ! console has a Tools tab for read-only inspection of the page you are looking at. Its commands mirror the inspection tools below: help , form [selector] , links [selector] , requests [limit] , logs [limit] , source [selector] , snapshot , history and clear . Results scroll in the tab and keep the newest 500 lines, help lists the arguments and selector examples, and the scrollback survives page loads. clear (or cls ) empties the Tools scrollback; the JS logs input accepts the same command to clear the JavaScript log. A [selector] is an optional CSS selector such as #content , .card , form#search , input[name=q] or main a ; omit it to inspect all forms/links or the full page source. Navigation, form editing ( fill / select / toggle / submit ), wait / wait_for and screenshot are available only through mob3 -mcp : the interactive event loop owns navigation results, so those commands cannot run synchronously from inside it. Tools Tool Arguments Returns navigate {url} Snapshot after loading snapshot {} Current page viewport, links and form controls links {selector?} Every link with index, text, href and selector screenshot {scale} PNG image of the current page viewport click {link} or {text} or {selector} or {x,y} Snapshot after the click form {selector?} Every form and control with CSS selectors and values fill {selector,value} or {fields} filled N field(s) select {selector,value} or {values} or {text} or {index} selected … toggle {selector,checked?} … is checked / … is unchecked submit {selector?} Snapshot after navigation wait {ms} waited <ms>ms wait_for {selector?} and/or {text?} , {timeout_ms?} found … after <ms> type {text} typed press {key} pressed <key> scroll {lines} or {pages} or {top} or {bottom} Scroll percentage back {} Snapshot forward {} Snapshot reload {} Snapshot evaluate {expression} JavaScript result as text history {} Session history, most recent first requests {limit?} Current page's request log and cache usage logs {limit?} JavaScript console messages and errors source {selector?} Raw decoded HTML, or one element's serialized HTML navigate Resolves input like the URL prompt: URLs, local paths, about: pages, or a search query through -search . Waits for the page, meta refreshes and scripted redirects. snapshot Returns the current page viewport as plain text: URL: https://example.com/ Title: Example Domain Viewport: 100x27 Scroll: 0% Example Domain ... Links: [1] More information... -> https://iana.org/domains/example Inputs: - input name=\"q\" type=\"search\" value=\"\" at (4,12) [n] is a 1-based link index for click({link:n}) . Only visible links are listed. Link numbers are stable layout indices, so a filtered list may skip numbers. Inputs positions are zero-based page-viewport cells, matching click . Page text is the viewport, not the whole document. Use scroll . links {} {\"selector\":\"main a\"} Lists every rendered anchor in the document, including links outside the current viewport, with the same [n] index that snapshot shows and click accepts: Links: 2 [1] Docs -> https://example.com/docs selector=\"a[href=\\\"/docs\\\"]\" [2] Sign in -> https://example.com/login selector=\"#nav a:nth-child(2)\" Empty anchors, hidden anchors and anchors with no rendered content are omitted. selector filters which anchors are listed (it matches the anchor, not its containers). Listings are capped at 200 entries. form {} {\"selector\":\"form#search\"} Lists every form and its controls, including fields that are outside the current viewport. Each control shows its tag, name, type, current value or checked state, flags ( required , readonly , disabled ) and a stable CSS selector for the other form tools: Forms: 1 [1] form method=\"GET\" action=\"/find\" selector=\"#search\" - input name=\"q\" type=\"text\" value=\"old\" selector=\"#search input[name=\\\"q\\\"]\" - select name=\"lang\" value=\"fr\" options=[\"en\" \"fr\"] selector=\"select[name=\\\"lang\\\"]\" - input name=\"safe\" type=\"checkbox\" state=checked value=\"on\" selector=\"#search input[name=\\\"safe\\\"]\" Hidden inputs are omitted because they cannot be edited. Controls outside any form are listed separately. Selectors prefer an #id , then a form-id name pair, then an nth-child path. Pass a form or control selector to list just that form. fill {\"selector\":\"#search input[name=q]\",\"value\":\"terminal browsers\"} {\"fields\":{\"#search input[name=q]\":\"terminal browsers\",\"textarea[name=notes]\":\"a note\"}} fill sets text-like inputs and textareas, replacing any existing value; an empty string clears the field. It accepts one selector/value pair or a fields map of selector to value. Values honor maxlength , and range inputs accept a number. Checkboxes, radios and selects are rejected: use toggle and select . fill never submits and never requires focus, so it also works on off-screen fields. Each change fires input and change . select {\"selector\":\"select[name=lang]\",\"value\":\"fr\"} {\"selector\":\"select[name=tags]\",\"values\":[\"a\",\"b\"]} {\"selector\":\"select[name=lang]\",\"text\":\"French\"} {\"selector\":\"select[name=lang]\",\"index\":1} Chooses by option value, visible label, or zero-based option index. Pass values for a <select multiple> to replace the selected set. A missing value or an out-of-range index is a tool error that lists the available options. toggle {\"selector\":\"input[name=remember]\"} {\"selector\":\"input[name=remember]\",\"checked\":true} {\"selector\":\"option[value=b]\",\"checked\":true} Checks or unchecks a checkbox or radio. Omit checked to flip the current state. Selecting a radio unchecks the other radios in its group, including groups outside a form. Multi-select options can also be toggled this way. Use select to choose a single-select option or replace a whole multiple selection. submit {} {\"selector\":\"form#search\"} Submits a form: runs the supported constraint validation, fires the form's submit handler, builds the GET query or urlencoded POST body, navigates, and returns a snapshot. Without a selector it uses the focused control's form, or the page's only form. Validation failures, invalid actions and preventDefault() are returned as tool errors (validation also focuses the first invalid field), so the page does not change on failure. wait and wait_for {\"ms\":500} {\"selector\":\"#results\",\"timeout_ms\":5000} {\"text\":\"No results found\",\"timeout_ms\":2000} {\"selector\":\".item\",\"text\":\"price\",\"timeout_ms\":5000} JavaScript timers do not run on their own between tool calls. wait advances the page for a number of milliseconds (default 100, maximum 10000), running due timers, applying the DOM rebuilds they cause and waiting for navigations they start. wait_for polls until a CSS selector matches an element and/or the given text appears in the rendered page. Text matching is case-insensitive, ignores markup and scripts, and sees the laid-out document rather than just the viewport. It returns found … after <ms> or fails with a timeout error; the default timeout is 5000ms and the maximum is 30000ms. When both selector and text are supplied, both must match. Both tools return a short confirmation rather than a snapshot; call snapshot afterwards when you need the page contents. screenshot {\"scale\":1} Returns an MCP image/png content block of the current page viewport, plus a short text line with the URL. scale 1 uses 8×16 pixel cells; scale 2 doubles the image. This is a terminal-cell raster, not a browser pixel screenshot: Go Mono glyphs are used, CJK/emoji glyphs may be missing, and MCP disables image downloads so image regions show alt text. click {\"link\":1} {\"text\":\"Sign in\"} {\"selector\":\"input[name=q]\"} {\"x\":4,\"y\":12} link uses a snapshot or links link number, including off-screen links. text clicks the most specific rendered element whose visible text matches, case-insensitively. Exact matches are preferred, then links and buttons over containers. It also matches submit-button value text and aria-label / alt fallbacks. selector uses a CSS selector. Links, inputs, checkboxes and buttons are clicked through their rendered cell, so normal browser click behaviour runs. A selector click scrolls an off-screen element into view first. x / y are zero-based page-viewport cells. Pass exactly one of the four targets. type and press type requires a focused input, textarea or the URL prompt. Click a field first. A newline presses Enter; a tab presses Tab. press accepts Enter , Tab , Escape , Backspace , Delete , arrows, Home , End , PageUp , PageDown and Space , plus single characters. evaluate Runs JavaScript in the page realm through sobek: {\"expression\":\"document.title\"} {\"expression\":\"JSON.stringify([...document.querySelectorAll('h2')].map(h => h.textContent))\"} Objects stringify as [object Object] ; use JSON.stringify for structured results. -no-js disables evaluation. requests , logs and source {\"limit\":50} {\"limit\":20} {\"selector\":\"main article\"} These expose the same diagnostics as the in-browser ! console: requests lists the current page's in-memory request log — status, method, size, kind ( doc , css , js , img , fetch , xhr ) and URL — followed by cache bytes used. limit keeps the newest entries (default 100; 0 shows all, up to the 512-entry cap). logs returns JavaScript console messages and uncaught errors in order, with · error in the header when the page has a script error. limit keeps the newest entries (default 200; 0 shows all retained entries). Logs and the error flag reset when a new page is applied and are lost on failed loads. source returns the raw decoded HTML that was fetched, before scripts ran. With selector , it returns the serialized HTML of that element instead. Terminal control characters are removed. Large sources may be clipped by the MCP transport. history Lists session history, most recent first; * marks the current entry. Typical agent loop navigate a page. snapshot to read text and collect visible link numbers; use links when the target link is below the fold. form to get every control's selector and current value. fill , select and toggle to set fields without focusing or scrolling. submit to send the form, then read the returned snapshot. Use wait_for after clicks and submissions that load content with timers, then snapshot when ready. Use click / type / press for special widgets, evaluate for DOM extraction, or scroll / back / history to move. When a page loads wrong, check requests for blocked or missing resources, logs for script errors, and source for the markup that was fetched. Limitations Text only. There is no pixel screenshot: MOB³ renders to terminal cells and has no font rasterizer. One browsing context. No tabs or iframes. Fixed height of 30 rows; only -width is configurable. JavaScript is a subset. Module scripts, workers, service workers and many modern APIs are unavailable. Forms. fill , select , toggle and submit handle text fields, textareas, checkboxes, radios and selects. File uploads and multipart forms are not supported, and framework widgets that rerender from their own state may overwrite a value set directly. Timers advance only during loads and waits. JavaScript timers run while a page loads and during wait / wait_for ; a timer that fires later needs one of those tools before its content appears. Unrelated tool calls do not pump timers. Memory only. Closing the server discards history, cookies and storage. Local, unauthenticated server. Use it only over a trusted local stdio channel. See Security and privacy for the broader security model.","headings":[{"id":"client-configuration","text":"Client configuration"},{"id":"protocol","text":"Protocol"},{"id":"debugging-in-the-running-browser","text":"Debugging in the running browser"},{"id":"tools","text":"Tools"},{"id":"typical-agent-loop","text":"Typical agent loop"},{"id":"limitations","text":"Limitations"}]},{"path":"docs/cli.html","title":"Command line — MOB³ documentation","text":"Command line mob3 [flags] [url|file] With no URL, MOB³ opens the -home target ( about:home by default). Input that looks like a search query instead of a URL uses the -search fallback. A local path becomes a file: URL. Interactive mode starts only when stdin and stdout are TTYs. Otherwise MOB³ falls back to dump mode and prints the rendered page. Flags Flag Default Meaning -dump off Render the page to stdout and exit -png off Write a PNG screenshot of the dump to stdout; implies -dump . PDFs render one page (page 1 unless -pdf-page chooses another) -width int terminal width or 100 Layout width in columns -height int 0 Layout viewport height and output limit; 0 uses the full page (dump/PNG/MCP) -pdf-page string auto PDF page for dump/PNG: auto , all , or a page number. -png defaults to page 1; a dump defaults to all pages -graphics string none Image mode: none , auto , kitty , iterm , blocks -g string none Alias for -graphics -no-js off Disable page script fetching and execution -no-pdf off Disable the built-in PDF text viewer and show the download prompt instead -reader off Start in reader mode -dark off Start in dark mode -ignore-blocks off Load anti-bot/CAPTCHA pages instead of the blocked page -bookmarks string none Import a Netscape bookmark HTML file -home string about:home Startup and Home-button target -js-memory int 256 JavaScript heap limit in MB; 0 disables the watchdog -cache int 32 In-memory resource cache limit in MB; 0 disables it -history-cache int 128 Back/forward history cache limit in MB of estimated page size; 0 disables it -mem-limit int 0 Soft process memory limit in MB for the Go heap; 0 (the default) uses the Go default -layout-budget string standard Layout limits: low , standard , high , none -zoom string 100% Layout zoom percent from 25 to 200; % is optional and + / - step the presets -text-scale string auto Kitty scaled text: auto or off -color off Force ANSI colors when dumping -no-color off Disable ANSI colors in dump mode -ua string MOB3/1.0 (+terminal; +https://mob3.net) Custom User-Agent -search string DuckDuckGo HTML endpoint Search URL template with %s ; empty disables the fallback -mcp off Run the MCP automation server on stdin/stdout -version Print the version and exit -h , -help Print flags and exit Run mob3 -h for the exact defaults in your build. Images and graphics -graphics=none is the default: no external image downloads and no inline SVG rasterization. When enabled, native terminal graphics are preferred: kitty graphics protocol iTerm2 inline images colored half-block art ( ▀ / ▄ ) Auto-detection uses environment variables. Under tmux, screen or zellij it falls back to half-block art because the multiplexer usually consumes graphics escapes unless passthrough is configured. -g is an alias for -graphics . Dump mode always uses half-block art for images that were loaded. An image with page text painted over it, such as a caption or a tab strip on a hero photo, is also drawn as half-block art: native placements composite above the text grid and would hide that text. Images stream in after the page text and appear progressively. A batch that only affects images below the current viewport does not redraw the visible frame, so the page stays steady while the rest loads; content below is laid out when you scroll to it. Color and dump mode -dump prints the whole document height (capped at 20,000 rows) without browser chrome. Color is automatic on a TTY, forced by -color , and disabled by -no-color . Output uses 24-bit color, except on macOS Terminal.app where it falls back to the 256-color palette because that terminal does not support 24-bit SGR sequences. With -no-color , interactive output keeps bold and underline attributes (links stay underlined and link hints use reverse video); dumps stay plain text. mob3 -dump -width 120 page.html mob3 -dump -width 120 -no-color https://go.dev mob3 -dump -color https://example.com Page height -height N sets the layout viewport to N rows and limits dump/PNG output to N document rows. This affects position:fixed boxes and other height-dependent layout. -height 0 keeps the full document height. The interactive UI always uses the real terminal height. mob3 -dump -height 40 -width 120 page.html mob3 -png -height 40 -width 120 page.html > page.png mob3 -mcp -width 100 -height 60 PNG screenshots -png renders the dump as a PNG stream on stdout. It implies -dump , is capped at 2,000 rows, and uses an 8×16 pixel cell with Go Mono glyphs: mob3 -png -width 120 bbc.com/news > bbc.png mob3 -png -g blocks -width 120 bbc.com/news > bbc-images.png Image cells render as colored half-block art. The renderer is text-oriented: CJK and emoji glyphs are missing from the bundled Go Mono font, and there is no browser chrome or true pixel screenshot. Layout budget -layout-budget bundles the style/layout deadline and fragment/document-row caps: Preset Deadline Fragments Rows low 1 s 100,000 80,000 standard 2 s 250,000 200,000 high 5 s 1,000,000 1,000,000 none disabled disabled disabled none can freeze the UI on hostile or generated pages. The preset is editable in about:config ; a zero individual limit disables that check. When a limit is hit, the page renders partially and the status says Page truncated (layout limit) . Page zoom -zoom 150 starts at 150% (values 25 to 200, % optional). In the interactive browser + and - step through 25, 50, 67, 80, 100, 125, 150, 175 and 200%, and 0 returns to 100%. Zoom changes how many CSS pixels one terminal cell represents, not the text size: body text keeps one cell per character and its normal line height, while fixed pixel widths, spacing, borders, images and kitty scaled large headings grow or shrink. The CSS viewport follows the ratio, so responsive pages reflow. It applies to dump, PNG and MCP runs, is editable in about:config , and is never persisted. In the interactive browser the URL row always shows the current level as a clickable badge at its far right; clicking it opens a window for choosing a preset with the mouse or arrow keys and Enter . JavaScript and cache limits -js-memory sets the per-script/callback heap budget. Every script also runs under a 2-second CPU watchdog. 0 disables the heap watchdog but not the CPU timeout. -no-js disables both script fetching and execution; <noscript> content is then shown. Press J (or click the footer JavaScript label) to toggle this for the session and reload the current page. -cache is a session-only LRU budget for CSS/JS response bodies and decoded images. Nothing is written to disk, and cache hits appear in the ! console. Home, search and bookmarks -home sets the page opened at startup and by the ⌂ toolbar button. The -search template receives the URL-escaped query in %s ; the default uses the server-rendered DuckDuckGo HTML endpoint. An empty value disables search fallback, so only URL-like input navigates. -bookmarks FILE imports a Netscape bookmark export (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge). The ★ Bookmarks toolbar button appears only when a bookmark file was loaded. Bookmarks live in memory for the session. Automation -mcp starts the headless MCP server instead of the terminal UI: mob3 -mcp -width 100 about:home See Automation with MCP for the client configuration, protocol and tools. about:config The ⚙ toolbar icon opens about:config , a form listing every CLI option and its current value. Editable options change for the session only; startup options are read-only; dump-only colour options do not affect the interactive UI. The top shows the current viewport in characters and pixels (using the terminal's reported cell size, or 8×16 by default). Nothing is persisted.","headings":[{"id":"flags","text":"Flags"},{"id":"images-and-graphics","text":"Images and graphics"},{"id":"color-and-dump-mode","text":"Color and dump mode"},{"id":"layout-budget","text":"Layout budget"},{"id":"page-zoom","text":"Page zoom"},{"id":"javascript-and-cache-limits","text":"JavaScript and cache limits"},{"id":"home-search-and-bookmarks","text":"Home, search and bookmarks"},{"id":"automation","text":"Automation"},{"id":"about-config","text":"about:config"}]},{"path":"docs/development.html","title":"Development — MOB³ documentation","text":"Development Requirements Go 1.27 or newer make no C toolchain: builds use CGO_ENABLED=0 dependencies are committed under vendor/ , so builds need no network Build and test make make test go vet ./... gofmt -w <files> make writes a static bin/mob3 . make clean removes it. make vendor regenerates vendor/ after a dependency change and must be committed. Tests must not require network access. Use the existing fake loader and httptest -free helpers where possible. Add or update tests in the same change as behaviour. Efficiency target The product goal is to cover most of the features people actually use while using far less CPU and memory than a mainstream browser. Concretely, when a choice is ambiguous, prefer the cheap approximation over the exact but expensive implementation, keep idle pages asleep, cap unbounded work, and never trade a large steady-state footprint for a marginal feature. See the working agreement in AGENTS.md for the rule in force. Measure locally, before and after, whenever a change touches the draw loop, timers, layout, the style pass or the JavaScript runtime. Comparing wall time, peak RSS and idle CPU on the same machine and the same pages is what matters; do not record hardware- or corpus-specific figures in the docs, because they go stale and invite false comparisons. make ls -l bin/mob3 TIMEFORMAT='%3R s'; time (for i in $(seq 20); do bin/mob3 -version >/dev/null; done) /usr/bin/time -v bin/mob3 -dump -width 177 -no-color page.html >/dev/null Peak RSS in -dump includes Go GC headroom and is higher than a settled interactive session, so compare like with like. For settled interactive RSS and idle CPU, run the browser inside a pseudo terminal ( script -qec \"bin/mob3 FILE\" /dev/null ), take the mob3 PID from pgrep , and compare fields 14+15 of /proc/<pid>/stat (utime + stime) over a window: an idle page must not consume measurable CPU. Per-phase parse, layout and live-heap attribution comes from a temporary zz_*_test.go benchmark following the debugging recipe in AGENTS.md ; delete it afterwards. Documentation README.md is the short landing page: what MOB³ is, how to run it, and what to expect. docs/ holds long-form documentation. mob3 -h and the in-app about:help page are canonical for flags and keys. docs/cli.md and docs/keys.md must stay in sync. docs/automation.md documents the MCP tools. Tool names come from mcpToolDefinitions() in internal/browser/mcp.go . The GitHub Pages site is generated from README.md and docs/*.md by .github/pages/build.sh , using pinned marked . Build the site locally: .github/pages/build.sh /tmp/mob3-site The script writes index.html , docs/*.html , search-index.js , robots.txt , sitemap.xml , the favicon and social-preview files under assets/ , a CNAME for mob3.net and .nojekyll . Relative .md links are rewritten to .html for the site. Docs pages share a generated sidebar, on-page “On this page” contents list and a compact search box at the right edge of the header, with Docs, GitHub, Releases and Contact links, a light/dark theme toggle after the search and a mobile menu. On phones the search is dropped so the whole header stays on one line. Every page carries a canonical URL, description, Open Graph and Twitter card tags and JSON-LD structured data, so search results and link previews use the intended title, summary and 1200×630 image. The assets under .github/pages/assets/ and the README screenshots under docs/screenshots/ are committed; replace og-image.png when the hero screenshot changes. Repository layout Path Purpose cmd/mob3/ Executable entry point internal/browser/ HTML, CSS, layout, rendering, fetching, JS, UI, automation and MCP docs/ User documentation .github/pages/ GitHub Pages template and build script vendor/ Committed Go dependencies bin/ Local build output (ignored) Key engine files are listed in AGENTS.md . The MCP and automation split is: automation.go — headless browser commands and text snapshots mcp.go — MCP/JSON-RPC framing; delegates to Automation.RunCommand cli.go — flags, dump mode, interactive loop and -mcp startup Workflows CI runs gofmt, go vet and tests on pushes to main and pull requests. PR requires the exact CLA statement described in CONTRIBUTING.md . Release binaries builds seven targets on v* tags. Docker publishes the multi-arch image after a successful release. GitHub Pages rebuilds mob3.net from README.md and docs/ when they change. Contributing Read CONTRIBUTING.md before opening a pull request. Contributions require the broad license-grant CLA so the project can remain available under AGPL-3.0-only or a commercial license. Keep the working agreement and engine decisions in AGENTS.md current whenever behaviour changes.","headings":[{"id":"requirements","text":"Requirements"},{"id":"build-and-test","text":"Build and test"},{"id":"efficiency-target","text":"Efficiency target"},{"id":"documentation","text":"Documentation"},{"id":"repository-layout","text":"Repository layout"},{"id":"workflows","text":"Workflows"},{"id":"contributing","text":"Contributing"}]},{"path":"docs/index.html","title":"MOB³ documentation","text":"MOB³ documentation MOB³ is a small web browser for the terminal, written in Go. It has its own HTML parser, CSS cascade, layout engine, ANSI renderer and pure-Go JavaScript runtime. History, cookies, storage and caches live in memory only. The project aims to cover most of the features people actually use while using a fraction of the CPU and memory of a mainstream browser. Warning: MOB³ is not a secure browser . It has no sandboxing, process or site isolation, CSP enforcement, certificate UI or security audit. Do not use it for passwords, accounts, payments or confidential data. Quick start Download an archive from GitHub Releases , or run the container: docker run --rm -it ghcr.io/mob3net/mob3 -graphics blocks Build from source: make ./bin/mob3 https://example.com Press ? inside the app for the built-in help page, and run mob3 -h for the full flag list. What can you do with it? See use cases for developers and sysadmins, AI agents and MCP, everyday terminal users, and automation and integration. The short version: browse over SSH, test and inspect pages headlessly, scrape JavaScript-driven content, generate PNG screenshots, run browser checks in CI, and give AI agents a local web browser.","headings":[{"id":"quick-start","text":"Quick start"},{"id":"what-can-you-do-with-it","text":"What can you do with it?"}]},{"path":"docs/install.html","title":"Install — MOB³ documentation","text":"Install Release archives Download the latest archive from GitHub Releases . Each release contains: mob3_<tag>_linux_386.tar.gz mob3_<tag>_linux_amd64.tar.gz mob3_<tag>_linux_armv6.tar.gz mob3_<tag>_linux_armv7.tar.gz mob3_<tag>_linux_arm64.tar.gz mob3_<tag>_darwin_amd64.tar.gz mob3_<tag>_darwin_arm64.tar.gz SHA256SUMS Every archive contains the static mob3 binary. Example: tar -xzf mob3_vX.Y.Z_linux_amd64.tar.gz sudo install -m 0755 mob3 /usr/local/bin/mob3 mob3 -version Windows users run the Linux binary under WSL. Interactive mode needs a real Linux or macOS terminal. Docker docker run --rm -it ghcr.io/mob3net/mob3 -graphics blocks The image is published for linux/386 , linux/amd64 , linux/arm64 , linux/arm/v6 and linux/arm/v7 . It runs as a non-root user and includes CA certificates, LICENSE and third-party notices. It is a terminal application: use -it to attach it to a TTY. MCP clients After installing mob3 , you can use it as a headless MCP server. OpenCode, Claude Code, Cursor and Claude Desktop all start it as a local stdio server: mob3 -mcp -width 100 about:home For OpenCode, one command registers it globally: opencode mcp add mob3 --global -- /usr/local/bin/mob3 -mcp -width 100 about:home See Automation with MCP for the OpenCode, Claude Code, Cursor and Claude Desktop configuration details. Build from source Requirements: Go 1.27 or newer make no C toolchain: the build is CGO_ENABLED=0 and produces a static binary dependencies are vendored, so no network is needed to build make ./bin/mob3 https://example.com Useful targets: make # static binary in bin/ make test # go test -mod=vendor ./... make vendor # refresh vendor/ after dependency changes make docker # local container image make clean # remove bin/ Set a release version locally: make VERSION=vX.Y.Z ./bin/mob3 -version internal/browser.Version defaults to dev ; release builds inject the Git tag through linker flags. The built binary is ignored by Git and make clean removes it. First run No configuration is written. The first run opens about:home unless a URL or file: path is supplied. Use -home URL to choose a different start page.","headings":[{"id":"release-archives","text":"Release archives"},{"id":"docker","text":"Docker"},{"id":"mcp-clients","text":"MCP clients"},{"id":"build-from-source","text":"Build from source"},{"id":"first-run","text":"First run"}]},{"path":"docs/keys.html","title":"Keyboard and mouse — MOB³ documentation","text":"Keyboard and mouse Press ? inside MOB³ for the built-in about:help page, which is the canonical key, mouse and form reference for the running build. Page navigation Key Action j / k Scroll one line down / up Space / b Scroll one screen down / up Ctrl-D / Ctrl-U Scroll half a screen g / G Top / bottom of the page Tab / Shift-Tab Next / previous link or form control Enter Follow the focused link, submit the focused field, or insert a newline in a textarea Ctrl-O / [ Back Ctrl-I / ] Forward (Ctrl-I needs kitty key reporting; legacy terminals send Tab, so ] is the portable key) r Reload R Toggle reader mode D Toggle dark mode J Toggle JavaScript and reload the page without or with scripts i Cycle graphics modes in place: full graphics, blocks, none (no reload) + / - Zoom the page in / out (25% to 200%). Fixed pixel layout, spacing, images and large kitty headings grow; body text keeps its size and line height 0 Reset zoom to 100% X Open the view options window (Fixed elements, Background images); Space / Enter toggles the selected switch o / Ctrl-L Open the URL prompt / Find in the page, or in the console HTML tab n / N Next / previous find match f Highlight letters in the visible links and follow one by typing them F Same as f , but show the destination and wait for Enter / y confirmation u Show the current URL in the footer E Open the full warning or error message that the footer truncated (the E in Error: is underlined) d Open the curl download popup for the current PDF page (PDF viewer only; does nothing elsewhere) y Copy selected text, the current URL when nothing is selected, the download curl command, or the whole active console section H Session history ? Help page ! JavaScript console q / Ctrl-C Ask to confirm quitting ( y / Enter quits; any other key cancels) :q Exit immediately (in the : prompt) Esc Stop loading, leave a field, clear a selection, or show the exit hint Back and Forward remember the scroll position you last had on each history entry. In-page # links become history entries too, so h returns to where you were before following the anchor, and Forward returns to the anchored section. If only the fragment changes, the page is not reloaded. Zoom changes how many CSS pixels one terminal cell represents, from 25% to 200% in preset steps. Use -zoom N at launch to start at another level. It is not text scaling: glyphs stay one cell per character and lines keep their normal height, so text stays readable while fixed pixel widths, padding, borders, images and kitty scaled large headings grow or shrink. The CSS viewport shrinks or grows with the ratio, so responsive pages reflow and percentage columns keep filling the terminal. Zoom lasts for the session and applies to every page, including pages restored from the back/forward cache. The URL row always shows the current level as a clickable badge at its far right; clicking it opens a window where a preset can be chosen with the mouse or the arrow keys and Enter . Console ! or the >_ footer icon opens the console. Inside it, : or Enter focuses the JavaScript input row, Tab / Shift-Tab or h / l switch sections, j / k and g / G scroll the active section, and y copies the whole active section (the logs, the raw page HTML, the request list, storage, or the Tools scrollback). Esc leaves the input row first and closes the console on the next press; ! closes it immediately. URL prompt o , Ctrl-L , : or clicking the URL bar opens the prompt prefilled with the current URL and selected. Typing replaces the selection; Backspace clears it first. : is a Vim-style command prompt: q or :q plus Enter exits immediately, without the confirmation that q asks for. History of earlier URLs and search terms is kept in memory for the session. Key Action Up / Ctrl-P Recall the previous URL/search entry Down / Ctrl-N Recall the next entry, or the draft Left / Right Move one character Home / End Start / end Ctrl-B / Ctrl-F Move one character Alt-B / Alt-F Move one word Ctrl-Left / Ctrl-Right Move one word Alt-Left / Alt-Right Move one word Backspace / Delete Delete around the caret Alt-Backspace / Ctrl-Backspace Delete the previous word or URL segment Ctrl-W Delete the previous word or URL segment Alt-D / Alt-Delete / Ctrl-Delete Delete the next word plus separators Ctrl-A Select all Ctrl-K Delete to the end Ctrl-U Clear Ctrl-C / Esc Close without navigating Enter Navigate A mouse click places the caret. Shift+drag is left to the terminal for native selection. Input is resolved as a URL first, then through the -search fallback. Find / opens the incremental find bar. Typing searches case-insensitively and highlights every match. Enter confirms and closes the bar; n / N then move between matches. Down / Up also move while the bar is open. Esc closes the bar but keeps highlights; a later Esc clears them. If the current match is inside a link, the footer shows (f → <resolved URL>) and f follows that link directly; clear the search to get normal f link hints back. The find bar supports the same word-editing keys as the URL prompt. Link hints Press f to follow a link. It marks the links visible in the current viewport by highlighting letters from the link's own text with a yellow background and an underline. Form controls are labelled the same way: text fields focus, checkboxes and radios toggle, a select opens its options, multi-select options toggle, and buttons activate. The chosen control stays focused for Space/Enter and arrow or h/j/k/l keys. The page text is never changed. Up to 26 links the first letter not already used by another link is highlighted (case-insensitive), so Documentation uses D and a later Dog uses o if d is taken. On link-heavy pages a hint whose first letter is unique still shows only that one letter; hints sharing a first letter use two adjacent letters, with no gap between them. Type the highlighted letters to follow the link; either case works. Press F instead of f to see the resolved destination highlighted in yellow in the footer and confirm with Enter or y before navigating; Esc or n cancels. If several labels share a first letter, the others disappear, the typed letter is no longer highlighted, and only the remaining letters stay marked. Unknown letters are ignored. Esc cancels hint mode, and scrolling with the wheel or scrollbar also dismisses the hints. Links outside the viewport are never labeled. A link with no text, such as an image link, gets free label letters drawn inside its box without brackets. While hints are shown, images are rendered as half-block art so the terminal's inline image protocol does not cover the hint letters. Forms Tab / Shift-Tab move through links and form controls in document order. A focused checkbox, radio, button or link is activated with Space / Enter ; arrows change radio groups, single selects and ranges, and move the active option in a multi-select, where Space toggles it. On radios, selects and ranges, h / l work like Left/Right and j / k like Down/Up. Click a text input or textarea to focus it. The focused field shows a block caret. Key Action Left / Right Move by character Home / End Start / end of line Up / Down Move between lines in a textarea Alt-B / Alt-F Move by word Alt/Ctrl+Backspace , Ctrl-W Delete the previous word Alt-D / Alt-Delete / Ctrl-Delete Delete the next word Backspace Delete backwards Delete Delete forwards Ctrl-A Select all; typing, Backspace or Delete replaces the whole value Ctrl-U Clear Enter Submit (or insert a newline in a textarea) Esc Leave the field Checkboxes and radios toggle on click. A <select> opens a popup; use Up / Down and Enter , or click an option. <select multiple> renders as a list box; clicking an option toggles it. A file input opens a notice that uploads are unsupported. Mouse Hover a link to show its target in the footer. Hovering also applies CSS :hover and fires mouse-over/enter events, so hover menus open and stay open while moving between padded items; style updates wait briefly for the pointer to settle. Leaving the page clears the hover state, and wheel scrolling never blocks on a hover rebuild. Drag to select page text; y copies it through OSC 52. With no selection, y copies the current URL. Click a link to follow it; a download link opens the curl popup without fetching, except a .pdf download, which opens in the viewer. Click the URL bar to open the prompt; click outside to close it. Click in the prompt to move the caret. Click the zoom badge at the right end of the URL row to open the zoom window and choose a preset. Click ← , → , ⟳ , ⌂ , History, Bookmarks, Help or ⚙ in the toolbar. Right click a link to copy its URL (OSC 52); elsewhere it does nothing. The mouse back/forward buttons navigate. Click Dark:on / Dark:off , Reader:on / Reader:off , JavaScript:on / JavaScript:off or the >_ icon in the footer. The wheel scrolls three lines; drag the right-edge scrollbar to jump. Shift+mouse reports from the terminal are ignored so the terminal can keep a native selection over the URL. Terminal-native selection needs the terminal's Shift override. Terminal support MOB³ enables the kitty keyboard protocol in detected kitty, WezTerm, Ghostty and foot terminals. That makes Esc , modified keys, Shift-Tab and key releases reliable. Multiplexers (tmux, screen, zellij) are excluded because their forwarding is inconsistent.","headings":[{"id":"page-navigation","text":"Page navigation"},{"id":"console","text":"Console"},{"id":"url-prompt","text":"URL prompt"},{"id":"find","text":"Find"},{"id":"link-hints","text":"Link hints"},{"id":"forms","text":"Forms"},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse"},{"id":"terminal-support","text":"Terminal support"}]},{"path":"docs/security.html","title":"Security and privacy — MOB³ documentation","text":"Security and privacy Warning: MOB³ is not a secure browser. It has no sandboxing, process or site isolation, CSP enforcement, certificate UI, phishing protection or security audit. Treat every page as code running beside the browser. Do not use MOB³ for passwords, accounts, payments or confidential data. What it does not do No sandbox, process isolation, site isolation or permission prompts. No CSP enforcement, certificate warnings or certificate management UI. No phishing, malware, tracker or fingerprinting protection. No proxy configuration UI. No private/incognito concept: every session is ephemeral, but pages and scripts are not isolated from each other inside the process. No persistence The running browser does not create or update configuration, history, cookie, cache, log or download files. Closing MOB³ saves nothing. History, cookies, localStorage / sessionStorage , the image cache and all JavaScript state live in memory only. The download popup only shows a curl command; it writes nothing. Disk reads are limited to explicit local file: pages and their resources, an explicitly supplied -bookmarks file, and /proc/self/statm for the Linux memory footer. OS-level reads by Go's TLS, DNS and timezone libraries are not application storage. Cookies and storage Cookies are session-only and shared by page loads, resources, XHR, fetch and document.cookie . HttpOnly cookies are hidden from JavaScript. SameSite is stored but not enforced. localStorage and sessionStorage are in-memory maps and do not persist across restarts. Cross-origin requests Pages can load cross-origin scripts, styles and images like a normal browser, and share the cookie jar for those requests. JavaScript fetch and XMLHttpRequest allow same-origin requests and only simple CORS cross-origin requests with a matching Access-Control-Allow-Origin ; preflight and credentialed cross-origin requests are unsupported. Blocked sites and challenges On 401/403/429/503 responses, MOB³ looks for common anti-bot/CAPTCHA signatures and shows a generic “Site blocked” page with the reason, status and URL. The site's challenge scripts are not executed unless you follow the one-shot Try anyway link, or start with -ignore-blocks . Challenge pages can run arbitrary script after that bypass. MOB³ cannot complete most browser checks, and it is not a security boundary. Local files and downloads Remote pages cannot navigate the viewport to file: URLs or load local file resources. Local documents can load local stylesheets, scripts and images. Only regular files are read; devices and FIFOs are rejected. MOB³ never saves downloads. A download link opens a popup with a ready-to-copy curl command without fetching the file, except for a .pdf download, which is fetched and shown in the text viewer. Running the curl command is your choice and outside MOB³. Automation -mcp runs a local, unauthenticated automation server over stdin/stdout. Any process connected to that stdin/stdout can navigate, click, type and evaluate JavaScript in the page. Do not expose the MCP server as a network service and do not connect untrusted clients. Sessions and storage remain memory-only. Media decoding Images and inline SVG are treated as untrusted input. Responses are capped at 12 MB, raster images above 25 megapixels are rejected before decoding, and SVG rasterization is capped at 1024 px. Decoder dependencies are kept at patched releases and scanned with govulncheck ; a panic inside a raster or SVG decoder is contained and shown as a broken image instead of crashing the browser. SVG strokes with a pathological dash pattern fall back to a solid stroke, and a stroke outline beyond a fixed point budget is dropped. These limits bound resource use and crashes, not the general risk of rendering attacker-controlled content. PDF responses are parsed in-repo rather than by a third-party renderer. The reader is bounded (500 pages, 4 seconds of parse time, 4 MiB of extracted text, 32 MiB per decoded stream), rejects encrypted documents, and contains panics, so a malformed file degrades to a note instead of a crash. It never rasterizes a page and writes nothing to disk. Like the rest of the engine it is not audited and is not a security boundary. Memory and caches The back/forward history cache is bounded by estimated page weight ( -history-cache , 128 MB by default): the least recently used pages are evicted first, and a page heavier than half the budget is never cached. When a heavy cached page is evicted, one collection plus scavenge runs between frames so its memory returns to the OS. -mem-limit sets an optional soft limit on the Go heap, making the runtime collect harder as memory approaches it. It is off by default ( 0 ), so the Go runtime default and any GOMEMLIMIT environment setting apply. These limits are best-effort resource bounds, not a sandbox. JavaScript safety limits Each script and callback runs under a 2-second sobek interrupt watchdog and a configurable heap budget ( -js-memory , default 256 MB). Timer counts and per-tick callbacks are capped. These limits protect against runaway scripts, not against malicious pages in general.","headings":[{"id":"what-it-does-not-do","text":"What it does not do"},{"id":"no-persistence","text":"No persistence"},{"id":"cookies-and-storage","text":"Cookies and storage"},{"id":"cross-origin-requests","text":"Cross-origin requests"},{"id":"blocked-sites-and-challenges","text":"Blocked sites and challenges"},{"id":"local-files-and-downloads","text":"Local files and downloads"},{"id":"automation","text":"Automation"},{"id":"media-decoding","text":"Media decoding"},{"id":"memory-and-caches","text":"Memory and caches"},{"id":"javascript-safety-limits","text":"JavaScript safety limits"}]},{"path":"docs/sites.html","title":"Site support — MOB³ documentation","text":"Site support MOB³ is a content browser, not a full browser engine. It is happiest with text-focused, server-rendered pages and gets rough on modern JavaScript apps. Works well Documentation, blogs, news articles, wikis, forums, changelogs and old or server-rendered HTML. Headings, paragraphs, lists, links, tables, <pre> blocks, images and common presentational markup. Responsive pages that use @media width queries, CSS cascade layers ( @layer ), basic flex rows and column flex media boxes, including framework columns with percentage offsets and the common box-sizing: inherit reset, basic grid tracks with explicit grid-row / grid-area placement, simple calc() and viewport-unit sizing, aspect-ratio ::before / ::after boxes, images sized with object-fit , CSS background-image photos on block containers, forms and simple JavaScript DOM effects. Older pages that declare a single-byte charset (Windows-1250/1251/1253/1257 and ISO-8859-2/5/7 and similar) are decoded correctly from the Content-Type header or <meta charset> . Fixed-width desktop layouts keep their declared canvas width even when it is wider than the terminal, so columns stay in place and the page clips at the right edge, like a browser window narrower than the page, instead of collapsing into one reflowed column. Classic bundles that only need platform APIs to exist: IntersectionObserver and ResizeObserver (one synthetic entry per target), NodeList , EventTarget , CSS.escape , customElements , ReadableStream , Path2D , Web Animations stubs and inert Worker s. ES module scripts run with their relative imports, dynamic import() returns a real namespace, and import.meta.url reports the page URL. Responsive and lazy images: srcset candidates, <picture> sources and common data-src / data-srcset placeholders are selected, and images added or changed by scripts are loaded after the page runs. Reader mode ( R or -reader ) for long articles. PDF documents A PDF response opens in a built-in, text-only viewer: every page is extracted and shown as one continuous document where each page ends with its Page n caption above an underscore rule. The reading column is centered and every page is padded to the same height, so the document scrolls like a printed book. Text keeps its column alignment, so tables and multi-column pages stay readable, and the result supports find, selection, -dump and -png like any other page. The footer shows Page n/N and a clickable (d -> Download name) hint; d opens the same curl popup. The viewer never rasterizes a page. Figures, images, charts, annotations and links are not shown, scanned PDFs have no text to show, and encrypted (password-protected) documents are refused. -no-pdf turns the viewer off and restores the download prompt; an unreadable document always offers the same curl command. Often rough Single-page applications, heavy shadow DOM, custom elements and CSS-in-JS. Sites that require a browser check, CAPTCHA, unusual TLS or a specific certificate. Complex grid/flex layouts, sticky positioning, z-index stacking, full float behavior, animations and generated content. Visual results from canvas, WebGL, video and WebAudio. Multipart/file-upload forms. Not supported Import map scopes, workers, service workers, WebSocket, IndexedDB, WebRTC, geolocation, clipboard APIs, notifications and browser payment APIs. Async generator syntax ( async function* ) and for await : the script containing them does not parse, so it is skipped. CSP enforcement, certificate UI and proxy configuration UI. Pixel screenshots for automation. MOB³ has no font rasterizer. Tips -no-js shows the no-script fallback, which is sometimes more useful. J toggles scripting and reloads the page for the rest of the session. R extracts the main article and removes navigation, sidebars and images. D asks sites for prefers-color-scheme: dark and keeps an author dark palette when the page already renders a dark background. -layout-budget low can keep a very large or generated page responsive. -dump -width 120 -no-color is handy for comparing layout without a terminal.","headings":[{"id":"works-well","text":"Works well"},{"id":"pdf-documents","text":"PDF documents"},{"id":"often-rough","text":"Often rough"},{"id":"not-supported","text":"Not supported"},{"id":"tips","text":"Tips"}]},{"path":"docs/use-cases.html","title":"Use cases — MOB³ documentation","text":"Use cases MOB³ is a terminal browser with a headless MCP mode, so it fits anywhere a graphical browser does not: SSH sessions, containers, CI runners, remote servers and AI agents. It is small and text-focused, not a full browser engine, so the best results come from content pages, docs and server-rendered HTML; see site support . Remember that MOB³ is not a secure browser : no sandboxing, no site isolation, no CSP. Use it for reading and automation, never for passwords, accounts, payments or confidential data. See security . Developers and sysadmins Terminal browsing — browse websites directly from SSH sessions, servers, containers and minimal Linux environments. Headless website testing — load pages, execute JavaScript, inspect rendered content and verify website behavior without Chromium. Web scraping — extract text, links, metadata and other content from JavaScript-driven pages. Website debugging — inspect how HTML, CSS and JavaScript render without opening a graphical browser. Screenshot generation — capture web pages as PNG images directly from scripts or the command line ( mob3 -png ... > page.png ). CI/CD integration — include lightweight browser checks in automated pipelines. Server-side automation — automate web interactions on remote machines without a desktop environment. AI agents and MCP Start with mob3 -mcp and the automation reference . AI web browsing — give AI agents the ability to browse and interact with real websites through MCP. AI research assistants — let agents navigate websites, follow links, collect information and summarize findings. Autonomous web automation — allow agents to fill out forms, click buttons, navigate pages and execute workflows. Local AI browser — provide a browser that runs locally, without sending browser activity through a third-party automation service. Agentic workflows — integrate web browsing into multi-step AI workflows and scripts. Website inspection by AI — let agents inspect rendered pages, discover interactive elements and understand page structure. Everyday terminal users SSH browsing — browse the web from remote servers without installing a desktop environment. Distraction-free browsing — access websites without the overhead of a graphical browser. Low-resource environments — browse on machines with limited memory, CPU or graphical capabilities. The project aims to cover most of the features people use while using far less CPU and memory than a mainstream browser. Privacy-conscious browsing — keep browsing history, cookies, storage and caches in memory only; nothing is written to disk. Inline image viewing — view web images directly in compatible terminals with -graphics=auto|kitty|iterm|blocks . Automation and integration Shell scripting — fetch and interact with websites as part of shell scripts, for example with mob3 -dump URL . Monitoring — periodically inspect web pages and detect content changes. Documentation lookup — browse online documentation directly from development environments. Link checking — visit URLs and inspect whether pages render correctly. Content conversion — turn rendered web pages into text or screenshots for further processing. Custom tooling — build other applications and agents on top of MOB³'s browser capabilities.","headings":[{"id":"developers-and-sysadmins","text":"Developers and sysadmins"},{"id":"ai-agents-and-mcp","text":"AI agents and MCP"},{"id":"everyday-terminal-users","text":"Everyday terminal users"},{"id":"automation-and-integration","text":"Automation and integration"}]}];
